CAT = "rbtEthics"; SEG = "SETH"
Q = [
# 2.01 mandated reporting
("During a home session, a 6-year-old client tells the RBT that an uncle hits him with a belt, and she sees welts on his legs. What should the RBT do?",
 "Follow mandated-reporting rules", ["Ask the parents about the uncle", "Wait for the supervisor's advice", "Record it in the session note only"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 2.01 requires RBTs to know and comply with mandated-reporting requirements. She also tells her supervisor, but cannot wait for advice or investigate herself."),
("An RBT suspects a client is being neglected, but her supervisor says to watch for another month before doing anything. The RBT's duty is to:",
 "Meet her own reporting duty", ["Follow the supervisor's advice", "Gather a month of data first", "Ask the family to explain"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 2.01 says RBTs comply with mandated-reporting requirements, and the code says to prioritize the law and the client's interest. A supervisor's delay does not remove her legal duty."),
# 2.07 client rights at risk
('At a clinic, the RBT sees a coworker lock a crying client alone in a room as punishment, which is not in any plan. What should the RBT do first?',
 'Protect the client and report it', ['Talk to the coworker after the session', 'Report it at the end of the day', "Ask the client's parent what to do"],
 "The client's safety comes first, then the RBT reports what she saw right away. Reporting at the end of the day leaves the client at risk; talking to the coworker later does neither."),
("A client's teacher keeps the client from eating lunch until he finishes all his work. The RBT sees this daily. Under the RBT Ethics Code she should:",
 'Report it to her supervisor', ['Bring the client a snack', 'Report the teacher to the BACB', 'Say nothing since it is school'],
 'The RBT raises a practice that may harm her client with her supervisor, who can act with the school. The BACB oversees its certificants, not teachers, so a report there does not help the client.'),
# 3.04 self-report within 30 days
("An RBT is charged with driving under the influence on a weekend. She is not convicted yet. Under the RBT Ethics Code she must self-report to the BACB:",
 "Within 30 days of the charge", ["Only after a conviction", "Within 24 hours of the charge", "At her next recertification"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 3.04 requires self-reporting legal charges within 30 days of the event or of becoming aware of it. A conviction is not required first."),
("An RBT is suspended by her employer for cause after a billing investigation. Which describes her self-report duty?",
 "Report to the BACB within 30 days", ["Let her employer report it for her", "Report it only if she is terminated", "Report it at her next recertification"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 3.04 lists disciplinary actions by employers, including suspensions for cause, as events to self-report to the BACB within 30 days."),
("An RBT is diagnosed with a condition whose medication makes her drowsy enough that she cannot safely run sessions. Which Ethics Code step applies?",
 "Self-report to the BACB in 30 days", ["Tell only her doctor about the condition", "Keep working and take more breaks", "Tell her clients' parents instead"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 3.04 requires self-reporting physical or mental conditions that may impair safe service delivery within 30 days, and 1.09 calls for steps such as telling the supervisor."),
# 3.07 certification status within 24 hours
("An RBT checks her BACB account and sees that her certification became inactive. Within what time must she tell her supervisor?",
 "Within 24 hours of learning it", ["Within 30 days of learning it", "Within 7 days of learning it", "By her next supervision meeting"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 3.07 says RBTs report a change to their certification status to their supervisor within 24 hours of becoming aware of it."),
("An RBT learns her certification expired last week. She has sessions booked today. Which step follows the RBT Ethics Code?",
 "Tell the supervisor, follow BACB rules", ["Run today's sessions and renew later", "Tell only the families she serves today", "Self-report to the BACB within 30 days"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 3.07 requires telling the supervisor within 24 hours of a status change and complying with BACB rules on practice, billing and use of the RBT title."),
# 1.10 multiple relationship with supervisor
("An RBT's supervisor starts asking her to babysit his children on weekends for pay. According to the RBT Ethics Code, the RBT should:",
 "Report it to the supervisor's manager", ["Accept it since it is off the clock", "Tell the supervisor directly it must stop", "Suggest a coworker babysit for him instead"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 1.10 says when a multiple relationship involves the supervisor, the RBT reports it to the supervisor's manager or another appropriate entity and documents it."),
("An RBT realizes her new client's mother is her cousin's wife, whom she sees at family events. What should she do?",
 'Tell her supervisor and document it', ['Keep it quiet if she stays neutral', 'Tell the supervisor in a few weeks', 'Quit the case without telling anyone'],
 'A possible multiple relationship is disclosed to the supervisor right away and documented, so they can decide how to manage it. Waiting a few weeks lets the conflict run unmanaged.'),
# 1.12 romantic relationships
("An RBT stopped working with a client's family 10 months ago. The client's divorced father now asks her on a date. Under the RBT Ethics Code:",
 "She must wait until two years pass", ["It is fine since the case ended", "It is fine with supervisor approval", "She must wait until six months pass"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 1.12 bars romantic relationships with former clients or stakeholders for at least two years after the professional relationship ends."),
("An RBT's romantic relationship with a BCBA ended a year ago. The BCBA has just been assigned as her supervisor. What does the RBT Ethics Code say?",
 "This supervision may begin now", ["She must wait two years first", "She must wait six more months", "Supervision by an ex is never allowed"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 1.12 says RBTs do not receive supervision from a past romantic partner until at least six months after the relationship ended. A year has passed, so it may begin."),
# 1.07 and 1.09 cultural responsiveness and bias
('An RBT notices she feels impatient with a family that speaks little English and has been cutting their parent updates short. Her best step is to:',
 'Raise it with her supervisor', ['Ask for a different family', 'Have the supervisor give the updates', 'Keep it to herself and adjust'],
 'Noticing a bias that affects her work, she raises it with her supervisor so they can address it. Handing the updates to the supervisor avoids the problem instead of fixing it, and keeping it to herself lets it continue.'),
("A family asks that sessions avoid their religious holidays and that the client not be offered certain foods as reinforcers. The RBT should:",
 "Pass the requests to the supervisor", ["Keep the foods since they work best", "Ask them to pick one holiday to skip", "Swap the foods for others on her own"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 1.07 asks RBTs to be culturally responsive working directly with their supervisor. Changing reinforcers is a plan decision, so she relays the request."),
('An RBT going through a hard divorce finds she is short-tempered with clients and missing data. The RBT Ethics Code expects her to:',
 'Take steps and tell her supervisor', ['Push through until the divorce is final', 'Tell the supervisor once it is over', 'Cut back on her data recording for now'],
 'When a personal problem affects her work, the RBT takes steps to address it and tells her supervisor now. Waiting until the divorce is final lets clients be affected in the meantime.'),
# 2.10 confidentiality in job communications
("An RBT emails the school team to schedule a meeting about a client. What should the email include?",
 "Only what is needed to schedule", ["A full summary of the plan", "The client's diagnosis and history", "Recent behavior data for context"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 2.10 says RBTs only share necessary client information in job-related communications such as emails."),
("A client's soccer coach calls the RBT asking how the client's behavior program is going. There is no release on file. The RBT should:",
 "Share nothing, tell the supervisor", ["Give a short update with no details", "Describe only the client's goals", "Confirm only that she works with him"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 2.10 says RBTs only discuss confidential client information under the direction of their supervisor. Even a short update is a disclosure."),
("An RBT is sick and texts a coworker to ask her to cover an afternoon session. Which message follows the RBT Ethics Code?",
 "Can you cover my 3pm at the clinic?", ["Can you cover Jake, the boy with autism?", "Can you cover Jake? He hit me twice today", "Can you cover Jake at 14 Oak St at 3pm?"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 2.10 limits job communications to necessary client information. The coworker needs the time and place, not the client's diagnosis, behavior or home address."),
# 3.03 cheating
('Before her competency assessment, a coworker offers an RBT candidate the exact role-play scenarios the assessor will use. She should:',
 'Decline to look at them', ['Look but do not memorize them', 'Use them only to practice', 'Decline, but keep a copy'],
 "Looking at the assessor's materials, or keeping them, undermines the assessment's integrity. She declines and does not keep a copy."),
("After her exam, a friend asks an RBT to write down the questions she remembers. What does the RBT Ethics Code require?",
 "Refuse to share exam content", ["Share only general topics", "Share them after results come", "Share only questions she missed"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 3.03 and 3.05 prohibit the unauthorized distribution of examination materials and misuse of BACB examination content."),
# 1.04 not the supervisor's employer
("An RBT who runs her own small business wants to pay a BCBA directly to be her required RBT supervisor. Under the RBT Ethics Code:",
 "RBTs may not employ their supervisor", ["This is fine with a written contract", "This is fine if the BCBA agrees to it", "It is allowed for part-time supervision"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 1.04 says RBTs are never employers of their supervisor. Only RBTs who are also trainees may separately contract for fieldwork supervision toward BCBA or BCaBA."),
# 1.06 and 2.04 competence
("A supervisor asks an RBT who has worked only with children to start next week with an adult client in a group home. The RBT should:",
 "Ask for training first", ["Start and learn on the job", "Use her child programs as is", "Decline the case outright"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 2.04 says RBTs do not serve unfamiliar populations without proper training, and 1.06 says to tell the supervisor and document it. Declining outright skips that step."),
# 2.05 restrictive procedures
('A parent asks an RBT to use a brief physical hold when the client runs off, since it worked at school. The plan does not include holds. The RBT should:',
 'Not use it, tell the supervisor', ['Use it only when he runs toward the street', 'Use it once and record what happened', "Follow the parent's request for now"],
 "Restraint is used only as written in the plan, so the RBT does not use the hold and tells the supervisor about the request. Following the parent's request, even briefly, applies a procedure no one has approved."),
# 1.11 gifts
('At the end of the school year a parent gives the RBT a $25 coffee gift card. Her employer has no gift policy. She should:',
 'Thank her and say no to it', ['Accept it since it is a thank you', 'Accept it and tell the supervisor', 'Accept it if it is the only one'],
 'RBT Ethics Code 1.11 bars accepting gifts worth more than $10 from clients or stakeholders, so she thanks the parent and says no. Telling the supervisor does not make a $25 gift acceptable.'),
("A family brings the RBT a homemade cookie each week as thanks. It costs little, but the RBT's employer bans all gifts. The RBT should:",
 "Follow the employer's ban", ["Accept since it is under $10", "Accept only on holidays", "Accept and share with staff"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 1.11 allows small occasional gifts but says that if an employer has a stricter policy, RBTs follow that policy."),
# 1.05 and 2.09 public statements
("An RBT's personal website says she is a behavior analyst who designs treatment plans. What is the problem?",
 "It misstates her credentials", ["It shares client information", "It lacks a disclaimer only", "It needs supervisor approval"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 1.05 says RBTs do not make false or misleading statements about their qualifications or services. An RBT implements plans under supervision."),
# 2.03 and F.9 professional skills
("In supervision, an RBT's supervisor shows video of her delivering praise late and asks her to practice. Which response shows professional skill?",
 "Rehearse and ask for more feedback", ["Explain why her praise was delivered late", "Agree but keep her usual timing", "Ask to skip the video next time"],
 "RBT Ethics Code 2.03 says RBTs take action to improve their performance after feedback. Practicing and seeking input reflect that, while explaining it away does not."),
]
